es there are a  variety of different interventions that  can be applied all of those are captured  at our algorithms and progressively  moving away from simple age based  recommendations to more personalized  than tailored suggestions related to  individual risk again why that initial  HNP is so important and here I highlight  the screening algorithms that we have  available some 10 15 different sites  about how we do it at MD Anderson in  case you're interested moving on now  downstream of screens what do we do with  screen positives were we're able to  offer three varieties of what we term  undiagnosed clinics for breast cervical  cancer as well as skin lesions this  results from our own screening efforts  or in many cases referred into our  institution from a local primary care  physician who's not a funny report and  they would like it considered by our  prevention experts so we offer same-day  quick appointments in our undiagnosed  breast clinic same-day consultation  intent and confirmatory testing we  typically try to refer a provide a  diagnosis within about two days and with  subsequent referral either to our cancer  prevention center if it's not found to  be a cancer or if it is in to one of our  treatment centers and again this is the  breast example but we have similar  clinical operations for cervical lesions  as well as skin lesions the last domain  I wanted to cover for you quickly is in  cancer survivorship I mentioned that  with fortunately the population of  survivors is growing in Texas in the  nation and around the world and we think  it's important to systematically pay  attention to at least four domains and  they're captured in this slide  first of all surveillance for occurrence  or second primaries third  I'm sorry second monitoring for late  effects of treatment  third risk reduction and early detection  either for a second primary in the  primary organ or at another site since  all cancer almost all cancer is strongly  age-related and then finally  psychosocial support to try to return a  cancer survivor back to the sort of  lifestyle that they had before the  diagnosis and this is just one example  of our breast survivorship algorithm and  you can see here the four domains that  are covered there again in a much more  detail than I can then I can provide for  you in this quick summary and we have  now 30 such survivorship algorithms and  this shows the full array of what's  available readily to the public and  available on the web should you have  interest I also wanted to point out that  aligned with the American College of  Surgeons Commission on cancer we offer a  cancer treatment summary to all of our  survivors when they're leaving their  clinical provider and this is  highlighting one such example of someone  who had a breast cancer in the past they  were treated by dr. Smith and Jones so  we provide a summary of their care and  we highlight in the passport what we  term a passport here is for another  either our own practitioners or another  practitioner outside of our institution  to pay attention to in the in the green  as well as the recommended surveillance  and prevention measures that should be  taken in the green and I'm sorry in the  purple and in the green respectively  we find that this is a very well  received by both the patients as well as  primary care physicians to try to assure  that the cancer care they received at  our institution is extended out both in  time as well as geography I want to  highlight the importance of attending to  the whole patient and taking a holistic  approach to prevention and health  promotion because just as this slide  captures for a breast cancer survivor'  you get out past ten years the greatest  risk is actually for cardiovascular  disease mortality not necessarily  recurrence and death from their breast  cancer so that holistic approach of  dealing trying to help prevention  broadly is very much on our minds  fortunately in the United States as of  2010 the Affordable Care Act really  brought greater attention and focus to  preventive service delivery and captured  here are the array of screening tests as  well as preventive services that can be  reimbursed because they are recommended  by the US Preventive Services Task Force  at an ARB level this is highlighting  that these should be offered to  individuals according to defined  schedules with no copay for anyone who  has insurance either through the  government or through a commercial  provider for example a commercial agent  for example so this is very important  because it really prioritizes and  removes one of the major barriers that  is cost around receipt of these services  for insured individuals we still have an  issue unfortunately in uninsured  populations but all of this has been  proven to be effective and I've  highlighted how we deliver these  services earlier in the presentation let  me just end by highlighting that this is  what we try to do but still achieving  prevention of cancer or other diseases  is a challenge both within a clinical as  well as the population context and  Harvey Fineberg  was a speaker here in our cancer  prevention Grand Rounds on our campus  about eight years ago and we asked him  if he would commit what he had shared  with us to writing and he subsequently  did and published it in JAMA in 2013 for  anyone interested in prevention this is  really a wonderful piece and I wanted to  bring it to your attention he highlights  the prevention is challenging because  unlike in a therapeutic context where we  can really change the fate of an  individual and  prevention there's no drama many times  we're talking about statistical lives  we'll never know if the action that we  take in the patient sitting in front of  us is really effective in decreasing  their risk our saving them from a  diagnosis of cancer that may be beyond  our interaction or of course we're  looking for something not to happen many  times for payers of preventive services  they complain that well we'll invest  today but the real result will come for  20-30 years thereafter and therefore  that investment baby lost it isn't worth  our investment in order to see that  long-term benefit they may accrue to a  different pair that's true but it's it's  terribly important to try to promote  healthy lifestyles in those within our  spheres another challenge advice changes  of course science moves on and we learn  new things so that's not a problem just  for prevention but it is a problem for  prevention because so much of what we  know about prevention is is of interest  to the general public so every other day  there's a new piece of data coming out  because of general interest in issues  like nutrition or physical activity  he highlighted that many times there's a  double standard in evaluation of  preventive and treatment services that  there can be commercial conflicts of  interest and sometimes prevention  practices may conflict with personal or  cultural beliefs and approaches think of  something like the importance of tobacco  to some Native Americans rituals that  that that can be very difficult to  overcome but the good news is he also  left us with a positive side there are  many strategies to try to overcome those  and other obstacles and some of what he  shared with us is captured here paying a  little bit of these for prevention  because many times people value what  cost them a little bit secondly trying  to make prevention cheaper than being  free so I already highlighted the  importance of the Affordable Care Act  and if we can even make it the easier  choice and something that doesn't cost  patients perhaps uptake could be more  consistent and profound across a  lifetime involving employers or other  payers very important trying to make  this a part of all of the lives of our  patients such as where they work pre  engineering system so that again the  healthy choice is easier to make and  then finally I'm 